"pcm (https://assets.press.princeton.edu/releases/gowers/gowers_preface.pdf)\nciteturn574view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 8; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://assets.press.princeton.edu/releases/gowers/gowers_preface.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 568\nL0@P0: ✐\nL1@P0: Preface\nL2@P0: 1 What Is The Companion?\nL3@P0: Bertrand Russell, in his book The Principles of Mathe\u0002matics, proposes the following as a definition of pure\nL4@P0: mathematics.\nL5@P0: Pure Mathematics is the class of all propositions of\nL6@P0: the form “p implies q,” where p and q are proposi\u0002tions containing one or more variables, the same in\nL7@P0: the two propositions, and neither p nor q contains any\nL8@P0: constants except logical constants. And logical con\u0002stants are all notions definable in terms of the fol\u0002lowing: Implication, the relation of a term to a class\nL9@P0: of which it is a member, the notion of such that, the\nL10@P0: notion of relation, and such further notions as may be\nL11@P0: involved in the general notion of propositions of the\nL12@P0: above form. In addition to these, mathematics uses a\nL13@P0: notion which is not a constituent of the propositions\nL14@P0: which it considers, namely the notion of truth.\nL15@P0: The Princeton Companion to Mathematics could be said\nL16@P0: to be about everything that Russell’s definition leaves\nL17@P0: out.\nL18@P0: Russell’s book was published in 1903, and many\nL19@P0: mathematicians at that time were preoccupied with the\nL20@P0: logical foundations of the subject. Now, just over a cen\u0002tury later, it is no longer a new idea that mathematics\nL21@P0: can be regarded as a formal system of the kind that\nL22@P0: Russell describes, and today’s mathematician is more\nL23@P0: likely to have other concerns. In particular, in an era\nL24@P0: where so much mathematics is being published that\nL25@P0: no individual can understand more than a tiny fraction\nL26@P0: of it, it is useful to know not just which arrangements\nL27@P0: of symbols form grammatically correct mathematical\nL28@P0: statements, but also which of these statements deserve\nL29@P0: our attention.\nL30@P0: Of course, one cannot hope to give a fully objec\u0002tive answer to such a question, and different math\u0002ematicians can legitimately disagree about what they\nL31@P0: find interesting. For that reason, this book is far less\nL32@P0: formal than Russell’s and it has many authors with\nL33@P0: many different points of view. And rather than trying\nL34@P0: to give a precise answer to the question, “What makes a\nL35@P0: mathematical statement interesting?” it simply aims to\nL36@P0: present for the reader a large and representative sam\u0002ple of the ideas that mathematicians are grappling with\nL37@P0: at the beginning of the twenty-first century, and to do\nL38@P0: so in as attractive and accessible a way as possible.\nL39@P0: 2 The Scope of the Book\nL40@P0: The central focus of this book is modern, pure math\u0002ematics, a decision about which something needs to\nL41@P0: be said. “Modern” simply means that, as mentioned\nL42@P0: above, the book aims to give an idea of what math\u0002ematicians are now doing: for example, an area that\nL43@P0: developed rapidly in the middle of the last century but\nL44@P0: that has now reached a settled form is likely to be dis\u0002cussed less than one that is still developing rapidly.\nL45@P0: However, mathematics carries its history with it: in\nL46@P0: order to understand a piece of present-day mathemat\u0002ics, one will usually need to know about many ideas and\nL47@P0: results that were discovered a long time ago. Moreover,\nL48@P0: if one wishes to have a proper perspective on today’s\nL49@P0: mathematics, it is essential to have some idea of how it\nL50@P0: came to be as it is. So there is plenty of history in the\nL51@P0: book, even if the main reason for our including it is to\nL52@P0: illuminate the mathematics of today.\nL53@P0: The word “pure” is more troublesome. As many have\nL54@P0: commented, there is no clear dividing line between\nL55@P0: pure and applied mathematics, and, just as a proper\nL56@P0: appreciation of modern mathematics requires some\nL57@P0: knowledge of its history, so a proper appreciation of\nL58@P0: pure mathematics requires some knowledge of applied\nL59@P0: mathematics and theoretical physics. Indeed, these\nL60@P0: areas have provided pure mathematicians with many\nL61@P0: fundamental ideas, which have given rise to some of\nL62@P0: the most interesting, important, and currently active\nL63@P0: branches of pure mathematics. This book is certainly\nL64@P0: not blind to the impact on pure mathematics of these\nL65@P0-1: other disciplines, nor does it ignore the practical and✐\nL66@P1: x Preface\nL67@P1: intellectual applications of pure mathematics. Never\u0002theless, the scope is narrower than it could be. At one\nL68@P1: stage it was suggested that a more accurate title would\nL69@P1: be “The Princeton Companion to Pure Mathematics”:\nL70@P1: the only reason for rejecting this title was that it does\nL71@P1: not sound as good as the actual title.\nL72@P1: Another thought behind the decision to concentrate\nL73@P1: on pure mathematics was that it would leave open the\nL74@P1: possibility of a similar book, a companion Companion\nL75@P1: so to speak, about applied mathematics and theoretical\nL76@P1: physics. Until such a book appears, The Road to Real\u0002ity, by Roger Penrose (Knopf, New York, 2005), covers\nL77@P1: a very wide variety of topics in mathematical physics,\nL78@P1: written at a level fairly similar to that of this book, and\nL79@P1: Elsevier has recently brought out a five-volume Ency\u0002clopedia of Mathematical Physics (Elsevier, Amsterdam,\nL80@P1: 2006).\nL81@P1: 3 The Companion Is Not an Encyclopedia\nL82@P1: The word “companion” is significant. Although this\nL83@P1: book is certainly intended as a useful work of reference,\nL84@P1: you should not expect too much of it. If there is a par\u0002ticular mathematical concept that you want to find out\nL85@P1: about, you will not necessarily be able to find out about\nL86@P1: it here, even if it is important; though the more impor\u0002tant it is, the more likely it is to be included. In this\nL87@P1: respect, the book is like a human companion, complete\nL88@P1: with gaps in its knowledge and views on some topics\nL89@P1: that may not be universally shared. Having said that, we\nL90@P1: have at least aimed at some sort of balance: many topics\nL91@P1: are not covered, but those that are covered range very\nL92@P1: widely (much more so than one could reasonably expect\nL93@P1: of any single human companion). In order to achieve\nL94@P1: this kind of balance, we have been guided to some\nL95@P1: extent by “objective” indicators such as the American\nL96@P1: Mathematical Society’s classification of mathematical\nL97@P1: topics, or the way that mathematics is divided into sec\u0002tions at the four-yearly International Congress of Math\u0002ematicians. The broad areas, such as number theory,\nL98@P1: algebra, analysis, geometry, combinatorics, logic, prob\u0002ability, theoretical computer science, and mathematical\nL99@P1: physics, are all represented, even if not all their sub\u0002areas are. Inevitably, some of the choices about what\nL100@P1: to include, and at what length, were not the result of\nL101@P1: editorial policy, but were based on highly contingent\nL102@P1: factors such as who agreed to write, who actually sub\u0002mitted after having agreed, whether those who submit\u0002ted stuck to their word limit, and so on. Consequently,\nL103@P1: there are some areas that are not as fully represented as\nL104@P1: we would have liked, but the point came where it was\nL105@P1: better to publish an imperfect volume than to spend\nL106@P1: several more years striving for perfect balance. We hope\nL107@P1: that there will be future editions of The Companion: if\nL108@P1: so, there will be a chance to remedy any defects that\nL109@P1: there might be in this one.\nL110@P1: Another respect in which this book differs from\nL111@P1: an encyclopedia is that it is arranged thematically\nL112@P1: rather than alphabetically. The advantage of this is\nL113@P1: that, although the articles can be enjoyed individually,\nL114@P1: they can also be regarded as part of a coherent whole.\nL115@P1: Indeed, the structure of the book is such that it would\nL116@P1: not be ridiculous to read it from cover to cover, though\nL117@P1: it would certainly be time-consuming.\nL118@P1: 4 The Structure of The Companion\nL119@P1: What does it mean to say that The Companion is\nL120@P1: “arranged thematically”? The answer is that it is divided\nL121@P1: into eight parts, each with a different general theme\nL122@P1: and a different purpose. Part I consists of introductory\nL123@P1: material, which gives a broad overview of mathemat\u0002ics and explains, for the benefit of readers with less of\nL124@P1: a background in mathematics, some of the basic con\u0002cepts of the subject. A rough rule of thumb is that a\nL125@P1: topic belongs in part I if it is part of the necessary back\u0002ground of all mathematicians rather than belonging to\nL126@P1: one specific area. groups [I.3 §2.1] and vector spaces\nL127@P1: [I.3 §2.3] belong in this category, to take two obvious\nL128@P1: examples.\nL129@P1: Part II is a collection of essays of a historical nature.\nL130@P1: Its aim is to explain how the distinctive style of mod\u0002ern mathematics came into being. What, broadly speak\u0002ing, are the main differences between the way mathe\u0002maticians think about their subject now and the way\nL131@P1: they thought about it 200 years ago (or more)? One is\nL132@P1: that there is a universally accepted standard for what\nL133@P1: counts as a proof. Closely related to this is the fact\nL134@P1: that mathematical analysis (calculus and its later exten\u0002sions and developments) has been put on a rigorous\nL135@P1: footing. Other notable features are the extension of\nL136@P1: the concept of number, the abstract nature of alge\u0002bra, and the fact that most modern geometers study\nL137@P1: non-Euclidean geometry rather than the more familiar\nL138@P1: triangles, circles, parallel lines, and the like.\nL139@P1: Part III consists of fairly short articles, each one deal\u0002ing with an important mathematical concept that has\nL140@P1: not appeared in part I. The intention is that this part\nL141@P1: of the book will be a very good place to look if there\nL142@P1-2: is a concept you do not know about but have often✐\nL143@P2: Preface xi\nL144@P2: heard mentioned. If another mathematician, perhaps\nL145@P2: a colloquium speaker, assumes that you are familiar\nL146@P2: with a definition—for example, that of a symplectic\nL147@P2: form [III.88], or the incompressible euler equation\nL148@P2: [III.23], or a sobolev space [III.29 §2.4], or the ideal\nL149@P2: class group [IV.1 §7]—and if you are too embarrassed\nL150@P2: to admit that in fact you are not, then you now have\nL151@P2: the alternative of looking these concepts up in The\nL152@P2: Companion.\nL153@P2: The articles in part III would not be much use if all\nL154@P2: they gave was formal definitions: to understand a con\u0002cept one wants to know what it means intuitively, why\nL155@P2: it is important, and why it was first introduced. Above\nL156@P2: all, if it is a fairly general concept, then one wants to\nL157@P2: know some good examples—ones that are not too sim\u0002ple and not too complicated. Indeed, it may well be that\nL158@P2: providing and discussing a well-chosen example is all\nL159@P2: that such an article needs to do, since a good exam\u0002ple is much easier to understand than a general defini\u0002tion, and more experienced readers will be able to work\nL160@P2: out a general definition by abstracting the important\nL161@P2: properties from the example.\nL162@P2: Another use of part III is to provide backup for\nL163@P2: part IV, which is the heart of the book. Part IV consists\nL164@P2: of twenty-six articles, considerably longer than those of\nL165@P2: part III, about different areas of mathematics. A typical\nL166@P2: part IV article explains some of the central ideas and\nL167@P2: important results of the area it treats, and does so as\nL168@P2: informally as possible, subject to the constraint that it\nL169@P2: should not be too vague to be informative. The original\nL170@P2: hope was for these articles to be “bedtime reading,”\nL171@P2: that is, clear and elementary enough that one could\nL172@P2: read and understand them without continually stop\u0002ping to think. For that reason, the authors were chosen\nL173@P2: with two priorities in mind, of equal importance: exper\u0002tise and expository skill. But mathematics is not an easy\nL174@P2: subject, and in the end we had to regard the complete\nL175@P2: accessibility we originally hoped for as an ideal that we\nL176@P2: would strive toward, even if it was not achieved in every\nL177@P2: last subsection of every article. But even when the arti\u0002cles are tough going, they discuss what they discuss\nL178@P2: in a clearer and less formal way than a typical text\u0002book, often with remarkable success. As with part III,\nL179@P2: several authors have achieved this by looking at illu\u0002minating examples, which they sometimes follow with\nL180@P2: more general theory and sometimes leave to speak for\nL181@P2: themselves.\nL182@P2: Many part IV articles contain excellent descriptions\nL183@P2: of mathematical concepts that would otherwise have\nL184@P2: had articles devoted to them in part III. We originally\nL185@P2: planned to avoid duplication completely, and instead\nL186@P2: to include cross-references to these descriptions in\nL187@P2: part III. However, this risked irritating the reader, so\nL188@P2: we decided on the following compromise. Where a\nL189@P2: concept is adequately explained elsewhere, part III\nL190@P2: does not have a full article, but it does have a short\nL191@P2: description together with a cross-reference. This way,\nL192@P2: if you want to look a concept up quickly, you can use\nL193@P2: part III, and only if you need more detail will you be\nL194@P2: forced to follow the cross-reference to another part of\nL195@P2: the book.\nL196@P2: Part V is a complement to part III. Again, it consists\nL197@P2: of short articles on important mathematical topics, but\nL198@P2: now these topics are the theorems and open problems\nL199@P2: of mathematics rather than the basic objects and tools\nL200@P2: of study. As with the book as a whole, the choice of\nL201@P2: entries in part V is necessarily far from comprehensive,\nL202@P2: and has been made with a number of criteria in mind.\nL203@P2: The most obvious one is mathematical importance, but\nL204@P2: some entries were chosen because it is possible to dis\u0002cuss them in an entertaining and accessible way, oth\u0002ers because they have some unusual feature (an exam\u0002ple is the four-color theorem [V.12], though this\nL205@P2: might well have been included anyway), some because\nL206@P2: the authors of closely related part IV articles felt that\nL207@P2: certain theorems should be discussed separately, and\nL208@P2: some because authors of several other articles wanted\nL209@P2: to assume them as background knowledge. As with\nL210@P2: part III, some of the entries in part V are not full arti\u0002cles but short accounts with cross-references to other\nL211@P2: articles.\nL212@P2: Part VI is another historical section, about famous\nL213@P2: mathematicians. It consists of short articles, and the\nL214@P2: aim of each article is to give very basic biographical\nL215@P2: information (such as nationality and date of birth),\nL216@P2: together with an explanation of why the mathemati\u0002cian in question is famous. Initially, we planned to\nL217@P2: include living mathematicians, but in the end we came\nL218@P2: to the conclusion that it would be almost impossible to\nL219@P2: make a satisfactory selection of mathematicians work\u0002ing today, so we decided to restrict ourselves to math\u0002ematicians who had died, and moreover to mathemati\u0002cians who were principally known for work carried out\nL220@P2: before 1950. Later mathematicians do of course feature\nL221@P2: in the book, since they are mentioned in other articles.\nL222@P2: They do not have their own entries, but one can get\nL223@P2: some idea of their achievements by looking them up in\nL224@P2: the index.\nL225@P2: After six parts mainly about pure mathematics and\nL226@P2-3: its history, part VII finally demonstrates the great✐\nL227@P3: xii Preface\nL228@P3: external impact that mathematics has had, both prac\u0002tically and intellectually. It consists of longer articles,\nL229@P3: some written by mathematicians with interdisciplinary\nL230@P3: interests and others by experts from other disciplines\nL231@P3: who make considerable use of mathematics.\nL232@P3: The final part of the book contains general reflections\nL233@P3: about the nature of mathematics and mathematical life.\nL234@P3: The articles in this part are on the whole more accessi\u0002ble than the longer articles earlier in the book, so even\nL235@P3: though part VIII is the final part, some readers may wish\nL236@P3: to make it one of the first parts they look at.\nL237@P3: The order of the articles within the parts is alphabet\u0002ical in parts III and V and chronological in part VI. The\nL238@P3: decision to organize the articles about mathematicians\nL239@P3: in order of their dates of birth was carefully considered,\nL240@P3: and we made it for several reasons: it would encourage\nL241@P3: the reader to get a sense of the history of the subject\nL242@P3: by reading the part right through rather than just look\u0002ing at individual articles; it would make it much clearer\nL243@P3: which mathematicians were contemporaries or near\nL244@P3: contemporaries; and after the slight inconvenience of\nL245@P3: looking up a mathematician by guessing his or her\nL246@P3: date of birth relative to those of other mathemati\u0002cians, the reader would learn something small but\nL247@P3: valuable.\nL248@P3: In the other parts, some attempt has been made to\nL249@P3: arrange the articles thematically. This applies in partic\u0002ular to part IV, where the ordering attempts to follow\nL250@P3: two basic principles: first, that articles about closely\nL251@P3: related branches of mathematics should be close to\nL252@P3: each other in the book; and second, that if it makes\nL253@P3: obvious sense to read article A before article B, then\nL254@P3: article A should come before article B in the book. This\nL255@P3: is easier said than done, since some branches are hard\nL256@P3: to classify: for instance, should arithmetic geometry\nL257@P3: count as algebra, geometry, or number theory? A case\nL258@P3: could be made for any of the three and it is artificial\nL259@P3: to decide on just one. So the ordering in part IV should\nL260@P3: not be taken as a classification scheme, but just as the\nL261@P3: best linear ordering we could think of.\nL262@P3: As for the order of the parts themselves, the aim has\nL263@P3: been to make it the most natural one from a pedagog\u0002ical point of view and to give the book some sense of\nL264@P3: direction. Parts I and II are obviously introductory, in\nL265@P3: different ways. Part III comes before part IV because\nL266@P3: in order to understand an area of mathematics one\nL267@P3: tends to start by grappling with new definitions. But\nL268@P3: part IV comes before part V because in order to appre\u0002ciate a theorem it is a good idea to know how it fits\nL269@P3: into an area of mathematics. Part VI is placed after\nL270@P3: parts III–V because one can better appreciate the contri\u0002bution of a famous mathematician after knowing some\nL271@P3: mathematics. Part VII is near the end for a similar rea\u0002son: to understand the influence of mathematics, one\nL272@P3: should understand mathematics first. And the reflec\u0002tions of part VIII are a sort of epilogue, and therefore\nL273@P3: an appropriate way for the book to sign off.\nL274@P3: 5 Cross-References\nL275@P3: From the start, it was planned that The Companion\nL276@P3: would have a large number of cross-references. One or\nL277@P3: two have even appeared in this preface, signalled by\nL278@P3: this font, together with an indication of where to find\nL279@P3: the relevant article. For example, the reference to a sym\u0002plectic form [III.88] indicated that symplectic forms\nL280@P3: are discussed in article number 88 of part III, and the\nL281@P3: reference to the ideal class group [IV.1 §7] pointed\nL282@P3: the reader to section 7 of article number 1 in part IV.\nL283@P3: We have tried as hard as possible to produce a book\nL284@P3: that is a pleasure to read, and the aim is that cross\u0002references should contribute to this pleasure. This may\nL285@P3: seem a rather strange thing to say, since it can be\nL286@P3: annoying to interrupt what one is reading in a book\nL287@P3: in order to spend a few seconds looking something\nL288@P3: up elsewhere. However, we have also tried to keep the\nL289@P3: articles as self-contained as is feasible. Thus, if you\nL290@P3: do not want to pursue the cross-references, then you\nL291@P3: will usually not have to. The main exception to this is\nL292@P3: that authors have been allowed to assume some know\u0002ledge of the concepts discussed in part I. If you do not\nL293@P3: know any university mathematics, then you would be\nL294@P3: well-advised to start by reading part I in full, as this\nL295@P3: will greatly reduce your need to look things up while\nL296@P3: reading later articles.\nL297@P3: Sometimes a concept is introduced in an article and\nL298@P3: then explained in that article. The usual convention in\nL299@P3: mathematical writing is to italicize a term when it is\nL300@P3: being defined. We have stuck to something like that\nL301@P3: convention, but in an informal article it is not always\nL302@P3: clear what constitutes the moment of definition of a\nL303@P3: new or unfamiliar term. Our rough policy has been to\nL304@P3: italicize a term the first time it is used if that use is\nL305@P3: followed by a discussion that gives some kind of expla\u0002nation of the term. We have also italicized terms that\nL306@P3: are not subsequently explained: this should be taken as\nL307@P3: a signal that the reader is not required to understand\nL308@P3: the term in order to understand the rest of the article in\nL309@P3: question. In more extreme cases of this kind, quotation\nL310@P3-4: marks may be used instead.✐\nL311@P4: Preface xiii\nL312@P4: Many of the articles end with brief “Further Reading”\nL313@P4: sections. These are exactly that: suggestions for further\nL314@P4: reading. They should not be thought of as full-scale bib\u0002liographies such as one might find at the end of a sur\u0002vey article. Related to this is the fact that it is not a\nL315@P4: major concern of The Companion to give credit to all\nL316@P4: the mathematicians who made the discoveries that it\nL317@P4: discusses or to cite the papers where those discover\u0002ies appeared. The reader who is interested in original\nL318@P4: sources should be able to find them from the books\nL319@P4: and articles in the further reading sections, or from the\nL320@P4: Internet.\nL321@P4: 6 Who Is The Companion Aimed At?\nL322@P4: The original plan for The Companion was that all of\nL323@P4: it should be accessible to anybody with a good back\u0002ground in high school mathematics (including calcu\u0002lus). However, it soon became apparent that this was\nL324@P4: an unrealistic aim: there are branches of mathematics\nL325@P4: that are so much easier to understand when one knows\nL326@P4: at least some university-level mathematics that it does\nL327@P4: not make good sense to attempt to explain them at a\nL328@P4: lower level. On the other hand, there are other parts of\nL329@P4: the subject that decidedly can be explained to readers\nL330@P4: without this extra experience. So in the end we aban\u0002doned the idea that the book should have a uniform\nL331@P4: level of difficulty.\nL332@P4: Accessibility has, however, remained one of our high\u0002est priorities, and throughout the book we have tried\nL333@P4: to discuss mathematical ideas at the lowest level that\nL334@P4: is practical. In particular, the editors have tried very\nL335@P4: hard not to allow any material into the book that they\nL336@P4: do not themselves understand, which has turned out\nL337@P4: to be a very serious constraint. Some readers will find\nL338@P4: some articles too hard and other readers will find other\nL339@P4: articles too easy, but we hope that all readers from\nL340@P4: advanced high school level onwards will find that they\nL341@P4: enjoy a substantial proportion of the book.\nL342@P4: What can readers of different levels hope to get out of\nL343@P4: The Companion? If you have embarked on a university\u0002level mathematics course, you may find that you are\nL344@P4: presented with a great deal of difficult and unfamiliar\nL345@P4: material without having much idea why it is important\nL346@P4: and where it is all going. Then you can use The Compan\u0002ion to provide yourself with some perspective on the\nL347@P4: subject. (For example, many more people know what\nL348@P4: a ring is than can give a good reason for caring about\nL349@P4: rings. But there are very good reasons, which you can\nL350@P4: read about in rings, ideals, and modules [III.81] and\nL351@P4: algebraic numbers [IV.1].)\nL352@P4: If you are coming to the end of the course, you may be\nL353@P4: interested in doing research in mathematics. But under\u0002graduate courses typically give you very little idea of\nL354@P4: what research is actually like. So how do you decide\nL355@P4: which areas of mathematics truly interest you at the\nL356@P4: research level? It is not easy, but the decision can make\nL357@P4: the difference between becoming disillusioned and ulti\u0002mately not getting a Ph.D., and going on to a successful\nL358@P4: career in mathematics. This book, especially part IV,\nL359@P4: tells you what mathematicians of many different kinds\nL360@P4: are thinking about at the research level, and may help\nL361@P4: you to make a more informed decision.\nL362@P4: If you are already an established research mathe\u0002matician, then your main use for this book will prob\u0002ably be to understand better what your colleagues are\nL363@P4: up to. Most nonmathematicians are very surprised to\nL364@P4: learn how extraordinarily specialized mathematics has\nL365@P4: become. Nowadays it is not uncommon for a very good\nL366@P4: mathematician to be completely unable to understand\nL367@P4: the papers of another mathematician, even from an\nL368@P4: area that appears to be quite close. This is not a healthy\nL369@P4: state of affairs: anything that can be done to improve\nL370@P4: the level of communication among mathematicians is\nL371@P4: a good idea. The editors of this book have learned a\nL372@P4: huge amount from reading the articles carefully, and\nL373@P4: we hope that many others will avail themselves of the\nL374@P4: same opportunity.\nL375@P4: 7 What Does The Companion Offer\nL376@P4: That the Internet Does Not Offer?\nL377@P4: In some ways the character of The Companion is sim\u0002ilar to that of a large mathematical Web site such as\nL378@P4: the mathematical part of Wikipedia or Eric Weisstein’s\nL379@P4: “Mathworld” (http://mathworld.wolfram.com/). In par\u0002ticular, the cross-references have something of the feel\nL380@P4: of hyperlinks. So is there any need for this book?\nL381@P4: At the moment, the answer is yes. If you have ever\nL382@P4: tried to use the Internet to find out about a mathe\u0002matical concept, then you will know that it is a hit\u0002and-miss affair. Sometimes you find a good explanation\nL383@P4: that gives you the information you were looking for.\nL384@P4: But often you do not. The Web sites just mentioned are\nL385@P4: certainly useful, and recommended for material that is\nL386@P4: not covered here, but at the time of writing most of the\nL387@P4: online articles are written in a different style from the\nL388@P4: articles in this book: drier, and more concerned with\nL389@P4: giving the basic facts in an economical way than with\nL390@P4: reflecting on those facts. And one does not find long\nL391@P4: essays of the kind contained in parts I, II, IV, VII, and\nL392@P4-5: VIII of this book.✐\nL393@P5: xiv Preface\nL394@P5: Some people will also find it advantageous to have\nL395@P5: a large collection of material in book form. As has\nL396@P5: already been mentioned, this book is organized not\nL397@P5: as a collection of isolated articles but as a carefully\nL398@P5: ordered sequence that exploits the linear structure that\nL399@P5: all books necessarily have and that Web sites do not\nL400@P5: have. And the physical nature of a book makes brows\u0002ing through it a completely different experience from\nL401@P5: browsing a Web site: after reading the list of contents\nL402@P5: one can get a feel for the entire book, whereas with\nL403@P5: a large Web site one is somehow conscious only of the\nL404@P5: page one is looking at. Not everyone will agree with this\nL405@P5: or find it a significant advantage, but many undoubt\u0002edly will and it is for them that the book has been writ\u0002ten. For now, therefore, The Princeton Companion to\nL406@P5: Mathematics does not have a serious online competi\u0002tor: rather than competing with the existing Web sites,\nL407@P5: it complements them.\nL408@P5: 8 How The Companion Came into Being\nL409@P5: The Princeton Companion to Mathematics was first con\u0002ceived by David Ireland in 2002, who was at the time\nL410@P5: employed in the Oxford office of Princeton University\nL411@P5: Press. The most important features of the book—its\nL412@P5: title, its organization into sections, and the idea that\n"
